  "about": [
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "About the Series"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "About"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "The people and the vision behind Origins."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Why We Made This"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "They were hunted, imprisoned, exiled, put to death, and still the movement grew. Anabaptist Origins is a documentary series that tells the first hundred years of that story, the birth of Anabaptism. Filmed across six European countries, it lets you see firsthand the council chambers that condemned them, the prisons that held them, the rivers and squares where they died."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "The People Who Tell the Story"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Behind the Mic"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Series Host"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Reagan Schrock"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Reagan Schrock co-founded Anabaptist Perspectives in 2017. Reagan graduated from Sharon Mennonite Bible Institute and completed a B.A. in Biblical Studies with Lancaster Bible College in 2021. Reagan also helped start an Anabaptist ministry that focuses on missions in unreached regions around the world. As a result, Reagan is involved in travel, teaching, and leadership with this organization (name withheld for security reasons). Reagan resides in Tennessee with his wife, Patricia, and two daughters."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Teacher & Historian"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Stephen Russell"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Stephen Russell is an instructor of theology and church history at Faith Builders Educational Programs. He earned a degree in European Studies at George Mason University (1986-1988), and an M.A. in Church History and Theology at Wheaton Graduate School (1989). His voluntary service experiences include two summers in Germany (1978, 1982), Choice Books (1981), and a halfway house for parolees (1980). He taught in Christian schools for five years and worked at Choice Books for seventeen years. During a seven month stay in Israel (2006), he wrote a book on nonresistance, Overcoming Evil God’s Way. The opportunity to teach at Faith Builders has been the culmination of a longtime dream."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Teacher & Historian"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Dean Taylor"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Over 30 years ago, Dean Taylor and his wife, Tania, walked away from military life after a life-altering encounter with the teachings of Jesus in Scripture. That pivotal season included witnessing the fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989—a moment that stirred both political and spiritual reflection. As they read the Gospels and studied the writings of the early church, they were drawn deeply into the historic Christian witness of nonresistance and discipleship. This blend of Scripture and church history opened a path that eventually led them into the Anabaptist communities."
    }
  ],
  "resources": [
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Go Deeper"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Resources"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "If you enjoyed the series and want to explore additional resources, check out the items below."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Full Timeline"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Want to dive deeper? Feel free to explore this PDF with many of the highlights from this era."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1415"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Jan Hus is burned at the stake"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "A century before the Reformation, a Czech priest is executed for challenging church authority — one of the first sparks."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "c. 1450"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Gutenberg’s printing press"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Movable type makes books cheap and ideas fast and becomes an indispensable tool for the Reformation and the early Anabaptists."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1517"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The Reformation begins"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Martin Luther’s protest opens a fault line across Europe that the Anabaptists will push further than anyone expects."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1519"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Zwingli begins the Swiss Reformation"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "As people’s priest at the Grossmünster Church in Zurich, Zwingli preaches through the New Testament and draws a circle of eager students."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1525"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The first adult baptism"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "In Zurich, a small group baptizes one another by conviction rather than infancy. Anabaptism is born — and made illegal almost immediately."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1527"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The Schleitheim Confession"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Seven articles set down what these communities believe. Their author, Michael Sattler, is executed within months."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1534 - 1535"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The Münster disaster"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "A radical faction seizes a German city and tries to force the kingdom of God by the sword. It ends in slaughter. The wider movement spends generations living down what a few men did in its name."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1536"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Menno Simons takes up the work"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "A former priest gives the movement a steady hand — and, eventually, his name."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1569"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "Dirk Willems turns back"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Fleeing across thin ice, Dirk Willems hears his pursuer break through. He turns back to save the man, is recaptured, and is burned. The story becomes the movement’s most enduring picture of enemy love."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1632"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The Dordrecht Confession"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Dutch Anabaptists set their beliefs down in eighteen articles. The document outlasts them, shaping Mennonite and Amish faith for the next four centuries."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "1660"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "text": "The Martyrs Mirror"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Thieleman van Braght gathers the stories of the persecuted into one enormous book. In plain homes it becomes second only to the Bible, a record of what faithfulness cost."
    }
  ],
